-- title:
--   Linear maps that agree on every irreducible subrepresentation are equal
-- statement:
--   Let $G$ be a finite group, $V$ a complex inner product space (no finite-dimensionality is assumed) and $\rho$ a representation of $G$ on $V$. Let $A,B\colon V\to V$ be complex-linear maps, not necessarily commuting with $\rho$. Suppose that $A$ and $B$ agree on every subrepresentation $W\subseteq V$ (a $\rho$-invariant subspace) on which the restricted representation is irreducible. Then $A=B$:
--   $$
--   \Bigl(\forall\,W\subseteq V\ \text{irreducible subrepresentation},\ \forall\,x\in W:\ Ax=Bx\Bigr)\ \Longrightarrow\ A=B .
--   $$
--
--   The statement expresses that $V$ is generated by its irreducible subrepresentations, a consequence of complete reducibility for finite groups over $\mathbb{C}$. It reduces the verification of an operator identity on an arbitrary representation to the irreducible case, where Schur's lemma and the classification of irreducible representations are available.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The inner product structure on $V$ is part of the ambient hypotheses but is not used. Subrepresentations are Mathlib's `Subrepresentation ρ`, and irreducibility of the restriction is the instance argument `Representation.IsIrreducible σ.toRepresentation`.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/OAI/RepresentationTheory/PartialPermutation/IsotypicProjections.lean#L22-L47

